%S A399785 2,1,0,0,0,0,0,0,2,26,240,481,2465,6189,43768,161752,999031,3535219,
%T A399785 12095155,35723365,55613923,116126711,138173976,205037454,451374272,
%U A399785 292341648,791672598,1619630519,3584172722,3608712098,4248995928,2636980367,2485437998,1376540254,6774136241,19281470851
%N A399785 Power chains: Number of essentially different permutations of the numbers 1 to n such that the sum of adjacent numbers is a perfect power, with each circular permutation counted only once.
%C A399785 This sequence counts all essentially different power chains, with reversals not counted as different: the linear chains counted by A399783, together with the power loops in A399784, each counted only once. Linear chains first occur for n = 7, while power loops first occur for n = 17.
%C A399785 Note that A399786 counts each circular solution n times.
%C A399785 This sequence gives the answer to the problem proposed by the French website Diophante (see link).
%C A399785 This sequence, together with A399783, A399784 and A399786, gives the four possible distinct counts of power chains (see Crossrefs).
%C A399785 In terms of graph theory, these numbers count Hamiltonian paths that cannot be closed to form a Hamiltonian cycle, together with Hamiltonian cycles, with reversals not counted as distinct, and with each circular solution counted only once.
%H A399785 Diophante, <a href="http://www.diophante.fr/problemes-par-themes/h-graphes-et-circuits/681-h103-les-chaines-de-puissances">H103. Les chaînes de puissances</a> (in French).
%F A399785 a(n) = A399783(n) + A399784(n).
%e A399785 a(7) = 2, since there are two possible power chains:
%e A399785       [1-7-2-6-3-5-4] and [4-5-3-1-7-2-6].
%e A399785 a(17) = 240, since there are 237 linear power chains and 3 power loops; the latter are given below:
%e A399785       [1-8-17-10-6-2-7-9-16-11-14-13-3-5-4-12-15], since 15 + 1 = 2^4,
%e A399785       [1-8-17-10-6-3-13-14-2-7-9-16-11-5-4-12-15], since 15 + 1 = 2^4,
%e A399785       [1-8-17-15-10-6-2-7-9-16-11-14-13-12-4-5-3], since 3 + 1 = 2^2.
%Y A399785 The four possible distinct counts of power chains: A399783 (number of linear permutations), A399784 (number of circular permutations), this sequence (number of total permutations counting each circular solution only once), A399786 (number of total permutations counting each circular solution n times).
%Y A399785 Cf. A001597 (perfect powers), A090460 (similar, but with square chains).
%K A399785 nonn,new
%O A399785 7,1
%A A399785 _Bernard Schott_, Sep 13 2026
